{
  "schema_version": 1,
  "list_id": "claude-opus-5-5",
  "run": 5,
  "simulation_id": "sim-04",
  "member_id": "mehlig-bernhard",
  "model": "gpt-5.6-terra",
  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_terra_round2_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B031",
        "laureates": [
          "Hidetoshi Katori",
          "Jun Ye"
        ],
        "citation": "for the invention and experimental development of optical lattice clocks"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B001",
    "B010"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "My configuration has changed from B001 to B031. Fluctuation theorems remain an exceptionally important and mature conceptual advance, but the discussion makes clearer that optical lattice clocks unite a clean physical mechanism with unusually strong experimental maturity and attribution. The magic-wavelength lattice removes the otherwise limiting differential light shift while permitting many neutral atoms to serve as coherent precision probes. Katori's foundational concept and Ye's decisive experimental development form a compact, complementary allocation. Independent replication and demonstrated measurements of gravitation and fundamental physics show that this is an established physical measurement regime, rather than a prospective technological promise.",
    "case_against": "B031 is still vulnerable to being described as a sustained metrological advance rather than one discrete discovery. The citation must therefore identify the magic-wavelength quantum-control breakthrough and its experimental establishment, not reward improved timekeeping, a possible future redefinition of the second, or the whole international clock-development programme. The exact historical boundary of indispensable credit to Katori and Ye also requires final archival scrutiny.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "kroll-stefan",
        "point": "Kroll argues that optical lattice clocks are already mature because independently reproduced clocks enable new measurements of gravity and fundamental physics, without relying on a future standards decision.",
        "response": "I find this resolves my principal maturity reservation. The relevant achievement is not a prospective metrological redefinition but the demonstrated ability to use controlled neutral-atom ensembles as extraordinarily sensitive physical probes. That capability is both reproducible and scientifically legible in a way that strengthens the case for a prize."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "johansson-goran",
        "point": "Johansson emphasizes that Katori's magic-wavelength concept and Ye's experimental development give B031 a focused attribution, while warning that the prize should rest on the underlying quantum-control advance rather than applications.",
        "response": "I agree. This framing identifies the essential mechanism rather than treating precision alone as the discovery. It also offers a more sharply delimited two-person attribution than my initial B001 proposal, for which the unity and priority relations among several fluctuation theorems remain more difficult to settle."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "danielsson-ulf",
        "point": "Danielsson holds that WMAP represents a settled observational transformation, while noting that some of the largest consequences of optical lattice clocks may still be developing.",
        "response": "WMAP remains a formidable alternative, particularly in maturity. But B031 need not depend on its largest future applications: the physical measurement regime is already established. Its more compact causal chain from a distinctive atomic-physics idea through experimental realization to replicated capability gives it the stronger present attributional case."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Whether historical review confirms that Katori and Ye alone encompass the indispensable conceptual and experimental contributions to the cited lattice-clock achievement.",
      "Whether the citation can maintain a sufficiently discrete atomic-physics scope while excluding subsequent performance improvements and broader applications.",
      "Whether the fluctuation-theorem priority and unity questions would ultimately make B001 the stronger fundamental alternative."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "I share the Gothenburg physics environment with Göran Johansson and Eva Olsson, but the supplied profile identifies no specific collaboration or candidate-related connection. No conflict involving the proposed laureates is apparent."
  }
}
